  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Workers&#x2019; Empowerment and Participation (WEP) Activity</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(Bangladesh):  Since the Rana Plaza building collapse in 2013, the GoB has made significant improvements in enforcing building and fire safety standards in many of the ready-made-garment (RMG) factories; however, working conditions and the rights of workers still remain problematic. In addition, continued modernization and automation of the RMG sector have reduced women&apos;s participation in factory jobs. Currently female workers comprise60 percent of the labor force in the export-oriented RMG factories. Domestic and gender-based violence has also increased as a result of loss of employment and income due to COVID-19. This activity promotes workers&apos; right to engage in union organizing as well as due process in the justice system. USAID engages factory workers, owners, worker-led organizations, women entrepreneur associations, international and local NGOs, private sector companies, and the GoB to promote labor rights, especially for women who face inequalities in the workplace. It promotes positive engagement with the factory management and independent workers&apos; organizations to advance workers&apos; rights in the RMG and shrimp and fish processing factories and in workers&apos; communities. The activity also provides legal assistance to workers and union members and supports unions by building their capacity to negotiate more robust collective bargaining agreements with employers. This IM meets the Labor ($2.7M) earmark. This activity is in line with the Integrated Country Strategy Mission Goal Two and U.S. government development objectives of improved democratic systems that promote transparency, accountability, and integrity and enhanced opportunities for an inclusive, healthy, educated society, and a robust economy.</narrative>
  </description>
